WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.840
In the era of chatbots, with every single company

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:06.320
having a chatbot that can access user data,

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.400
there's a lot of new attack surface.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.960
And I don't only mean AI-related attacks

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.600
like prompt injection.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.080
It's even a lot of new regular web vulnerabilities

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.200
that arise.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:20.040
It's especially dangerous when these chatbots

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.200
can access critical user information,

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.559
like the Azure HealthBot service

00:00:25.559 --> 00:00:28.719
that is created to answer questions of patients

00:00:28.719 --> 00:00:31.600
based on their medical history.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.599
A hunter called Yanir Tsarimi found a total of four bugs,

00:00:36.799 --> 00:00:39.240
allowing him to steal data of other users,

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:44.240
for which he was awarded a total of $203,000.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.040
I will explain this in this video.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:48.680
Enjoy.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.599
Azure HealthBot is a service that can respond

00:00:51.599 --> 00:00:55.720
to customers' basic questions based on their medical database

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:58.560
and based on their medical history.

00:00:58.599 --> 00:01:02.599
It was actually built pre-ChatGPT or pre-LLM era,

00:01:02.599 --> 00:01:07.040
so it does not everything based on the LLM.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.480
For example, you can control the flow of the conversation

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.720
using just different expressions in JavaScript.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.360
And of course, this is meant to be used

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:18.760
by hospital and medical facilities.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.480
So then the attack scenario,

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.400
you could think the attack scenario

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.279
is only one hospital attacking another,

00:01:26.279 --> 00:01:28.760
but the truth is everyone can go to Azure website

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:31.639
and create their own Azure HealthBot.

00:01:31.639 --> 00:01:34.080
So the attacker can do it as well.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.360
So the attack scenario of one tenant

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.919
attacking data of another is very, very critical

00:01:39.919 --> 00:01:42.160
and can be very dangerous.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.519
But of course, developers were aware of this risk

00:01:45.519 --> 00:01:47.239
because as I've told you,

00:01:47.239 --> 00:01:50.879
you can create your own JavaScript expressions

00:01:50.879 --> 00:01:53.000
to control the flow of the conversation,

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:56.760
but to mitigate the risk of the JavaScript code

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.040
just reading data of other users,

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.440
this code runs in the isolated environment

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:04.959
using the VM tool library.

00:02:04.959 --> 00:02:06.959
The way it works is it overrides

00:02:06.959 --> 00:02:09.039
a lot of built-in JavaScript functions

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:10.800
and access to the prototype

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.520
so that you cannot execute whatever code you want.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.000
For example, the require function,

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.320
which is used to import modules,

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:23.520
is overridden so that you cannot import any module.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.800
And this is robust, this is secure,

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.479
but Microsoft wanted to change it slightly.

00:02:28.479 --> 00:02:32.759
They wanted to disallow importing most of the modules

00:02:32.759 --> 00:02:35.960
while still being able to import some of them.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:39.600
So they overridden the require function again,

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.080
and this is the code that they used.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.839
At the first sight, it looks pretty secure.

00:02:44.839 --> 00:02:47.839
It checks if there's a whitelist of modules,

00:02:47.839 --> 00:02:49.360
and if there is, it checks

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:53.240
if the currently imported module is on that whitelist.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:57.320
We don't have access to modify the whitelist,

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:58.960
so there's not much we can do.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:03.520
There's only one function called to the index of function.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.039
Can you override the index of function?

00:03:06.039 --> 00:03:07.320
Well, actually you can.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.160
If you start developer tools right now,

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.160
you can, using this code,

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.639
you can override the index of function

00:03:14.639 --> 00:03:16.440
to do whatever you want.

00:03:16.479 --> 00:03:19.000
But the thing is, one of the main features

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.960
of the VM2 library is to restrict the access

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:25.160
to the prototype, so this would not work

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:27.720
in the VM2 environment.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.399
But if you take a look at this code again,

00:03:30.399 --> 00:03:33.520
it's not the built-in index of function that was used,

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:36.199
but instead is the index of function

00:03:36.199 --> 00:03:38.839
from the underscore library.

00:03:38.839 --> 00:03:42.080
And access to this is not restricted at all,

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:45.360
so using this code, you could override

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.000
the index of function to always return a number.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.119
So this check would always be true for any module,

00:03:52.119 --> 00:03:54.919
even those from outside this allow list,

00:03:54.919 --> 00:03:58.880
which allowed you to import any module,

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.880
which then allowed you to execute arbitrary code,

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:05.960
which you could use to steal data of other patients.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:09.080
And after Microsoft fixed this particular flaws,

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:12.000
the hunter did not find a way to bypass this.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.479
He did not find a way to import modules

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:18.480
from outside the allow list, so then he decided,

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.920
okay, I cannot import any other modules,

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:23.760
so let's see if I can do anything interesting

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:25.720
with those that are allow listed.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:29.839
Because the way VM2 works is the code

00:04:29.839 --> 00:04:32.359
of those allow listed modules is run

00:04:32.359 --> 00:04:34.200
in the unisolated context,

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.239
which means it's not restricted at all,

00:04:36.239 --> 00:04:39.200
which means you do not want to be running

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:40.799
any user code there.

00:04:40.799 --> 00:04:43.200
Why does it work in this particular way?

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:44.359
To be honest, I'm not sure,

00:04:44.359 --> 00:04:48.640
but maybe it was due to a lot of dependencies.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:51.959
If you wanted to run the module in the isolated context,

00:04:51.959 --> 00:04:53.600
you would also have to allow list

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:55.880
all the dependencies of this module.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.880
Maybe it was something like this, I'm not sure,

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:00.559
but it's also worth pointing out

00:05:00.559 --> 00:05:02.839
that the whole VM2 library,

00:05:02.839 --> 00:05:05.320
if you go to your NPM page,

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:08.000
it says it's deprecated and it contains

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:11.279
known sandbox escapes or isolation escapes

00:05:11.279 --> 00:05:13.119
or whatever you want to call it.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:14.720
And if you go to GitHub,

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:17.279
you can literally see two escapes

00:05:17.279 --> 00:05:20.279
that were found in 2023 that are not fixed

00:05:20.279 --> 00:05:22.799
and that will not be fixed.

00:05:22.799 --> 00:05:24.600
So I'm quite interested,

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.799
how did Microsoft deal with these?

00:05:27.799 --> 00:05:29.839
But coming back to our case,

00:05:29.839 --> 00:05:33.160
to the blog post by the hunter,

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:37.399
he found that one of the import allow listed libraries

00:05:37.399 --> 00:05:39.880
was underscore, which is the same library

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:41.799
he exploited in the first step.

00:05:41.839 --> 00:05:44.440
He found that the underscore library

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:46.640
contains the template function,

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:48.559
which just consumes a template,

00:05:48.559 --> 00:05:51.119
which can contain JavaScript code.

00:05:51.119 --> 00:05:54.079
So it's a pretty straightforward code execution.

00:05:54.079 --> 00:05:55.640
And as I've told you before,

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:59.399
this code is run in the unisolated context,

00:05:59.399 --> 00:06:02.760
which means you can just read data of other tenants,

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:07.079
read data of other patients and send it to you.

00:06:07.079 --> 00:06:10.799
So it's another way to execute code.

00:06:10.799 --> 00:06:13.799
So Microsoft fixed this one as well.

00:06:13.799 --> 00:06:17.320
And the hunter no longer could import arbitrary modules.

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:20.880
He also didn't find another way to execute code.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:24.079
So he decided to look at other ways to leak data

00:06:24.079 --> 00:06:25.239
because after all,

00:06:25.239 --> 00:06:29.920
executing arbitrary code is not the only way to leak data.

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:34.359
And he found that he can unsafely allocate a buffer

00:06:34.359 --> 00:06:37.959
because normally when you do this with a call like this,

00:06:37.959 --> 00:06:40.559
what happens is the buffer is zero out.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:43.279
The memory in the buffer is zeroed out,

00:06:43.279 --> 00:06:46.720
which means if you read it just after initialization,

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:49.480
it's empty, it contains only zeros.

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:51.679
But if you do exactly the same thing

00:06:51.679 --> 00:06:55.279
with the unsafe methods to allocate a buffer,

00:06:55.279 --> 00:06:56.600
it's basically the same

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:58.880
just without the memory zeroing step,

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:02.839
which means you are reading a random part of the memory

00:07:02.839 --> 00:07:05.000
with some data that was processed

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.679
fairly recently by the server.

00:07:07.679 --> 00:07:10.200
And when he repeated this process a few times,

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:14.760
he would find JWT tokens from internal Azure identity codes.

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:16.839
He would find Kubernetes codes.

00:07:16.839 --> 00:07:21.279
He found data of the customers of another hospitals

00:07:21.279 --> 00:07:22.600
and stuff like this.

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:25.480
So even though it didn't allow him to execute code,

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:29.839
he could read a lot of sensitive information in this way.

00:07:30.839 --> 00:07:32.000
And for the fourth one,

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:34.920
which is to be honest, my favorite of these four,

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:36.359
we'll step a little bit back.

00:07:36.359 --> 00:07:40.640
We'll let go of the code execution functionality

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:42.040
and we'll take a look

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:44.920
at how did this bot fetch data of users.

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:49.799
And this is the URL which would be sent to fetch this data.

00:07:49.799 --> 00:07:54.320
Importantly, three of these parts of the URL are static

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:56.720
and two of them are parameters.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:58.839
One of these parameters is the tenant name,

00:07:58.839 --> 00:08:00.600
which would be the name of your tenant

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:03.920
and the other would be ID of the particular row

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:05.399
that you want to fetch.

00:08:05.399 --> 00:08:08.239
And at this point, there was the authorization check.

00:08:08.239 --> 00:08:12.160
If the tenant ID that's in the URL is your tenant ID

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:14.160
or the tenant you're authorized to.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:18.040
If you try to put the name of another hospital here

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:21.160
or another medical facility here, it would not work.

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:23.839
If the authorization check was good,

00:08:23.839 --> 00:08:26.839
it would proceed to make this underlying call.

00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:28.279
You wouldn't see this as a user.

00:08:28.279 --> 00:08:30.640
It would be somewhere on the backend,

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:33.159
but this is the URL that would be constructed.

00:08:33.159 --> 00:08:35.559
And kudos to researcher for finding this out

00:08:35.559 --> 00:08:37.640
because likely he could not see this.

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:42.080
He could just guess it's what happens under the hood.

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:46.919
And this is the URL that's Azure table API call.

00:08:46.919 --> 00:08:48.880
And again, we have the tenant ID

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:50.919
and then we have the row ID

00:08:50.919 --> 00:08:53.159
which was sent in the previous step.

00:08:53.159 --> 00:08:56.840
Then when this call is made, the user data is returned

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:59.919
and the user data is then forwarded back to you

00:08:59.919 --> 00:09:02.400
or back to your bot to be more precise.

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:04.080
So now think what would happen

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:07.799
if you send the request to a URL like this.

00:09:07.799 --> 00:09:10.119
The authorization check would still be okay

00:09:10.119 --> 00:09:12.840
because it's still the tenant parameter.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:14.760
It still points to your tenant.

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:17.919
So you wouldn't be denied access to this.

00:09:17.919 --> 00:09:20.760
However, after this, in the second step,

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:23.440
when the internal request would be sent,

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:26.520
this is the URL that would be constructed.

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:30.640
And this part would be canceled due to the path traversal.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:34.200
So this URL would be equivalent to this one,

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:38.799
which is a URL that fetches data of another customer.

00:09:38.799 --> 00:09:42.000
And then this response is sent back to you,

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:45.599
thus giving you data of arbitrary tenant

00:09:45.599 --> 00:09:47.440
of Azure Health Bot.

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:49.960
We do not know what were the exact bounties

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:52.080
awarded for these four vulnerabilities,

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:57.080
but we know that the total was $203,000.

00:09:57.400 --> 00:09:59.280
And that's it for today's video.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:02.080
If you've learned something new, leave a like.

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:03.919
And if you want to watch another video

00:10:03.919 --> 00:10:05.559
about a path traversal,

00:10:05.559 --> 00:10:09.119
check out this one about my $20,000 path traversal

00:10:09.119 --> 00:10:11.359
in the Amazon S3 integration.

00:10:11.359 --> 00:10:14.520
For now, thank you for watching and goodbye.

